course: dictionary
lesson: 14153
title: Misrepresent: when the picture is wrong
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To misrepresent something means to describe it in a false or unfair way, so that other people get the wrong idea. The word is built from mis- (wrong) and represent (describe or show). «The advert showed the small bag as if it were a huge suitcase.» The advert misrepresents the bag, because the picture gives a false idea of its size.
explain: You can also misrepresent yourself. This means pretending to be something you are not. «Sam typed that he was fourteen on the game website, but he is only ten.» Sam misrepresented his age, which is why online safety rules matter.
explain: Look for clues in the words around it. Words like false, unfair, wrong or left out tell you that something has been shown the wrong way. When you read the news, an advert or what someone said, ask: is this a fair picture of what really happened?
fact: To misrepresent means to describe someone or something in a false or unfair way.
fact: «Misrepresent» is built from mis- (wrong) and represent (describe or show).
fact: «Misrepresent the facts» means giving a false picture of what is true.
fact: «Be misrepresented» means someone has been described unfairly, so others get the wrong idea.
fact: The noun is misrepresentation, which means a false or unfair description.
exercise: «The news story left out what the referee said, so it gave a false picture of the match.» What does misrepresent mean here? | *Describe something in a false or unfair way | Paint something a new colour | Make something louder | Describe something exactly as it is
exercise: Riya says misrepresent means the same as tell the truth. Is she right? | Yes, it means to say sorry | No, it means to go slowly | Yes, it means to tell the truth clearly | *No, it means the opposite: giving a false or unfair picture
exercise: «Please do not misrepresent my words. I said we should wait, not stop.» What is the speaker asking for? | Their words to be written in capital letters | Their words to be forgotten | *Their words to be reported fairly and not changed | Their words to be sung as a song
test: Mia pretends to be a grown-up on a chat site. What is she doing? | *Misrepresenting herself | Making a cake for a friend | Telling the truth about her hobbies | Sending a photo of her dog
test: «The paper printed only half of the mayor's speech, so it made her sound angry.» What has the paper done? | Quoted the mayor fully and fairly | Thanked the mayor for her work | *Misrepresented the mayor | Made the speech shorter for the readers
exam: The newspaper quoted the mayor as saying «I will close the park», but she only said she would look at it. What has the newspaper done? | Given the park a new name | Called the park beautiful | *Misrepresented her words | Quoted her correctly
